WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.955 --> 00:00:12.835
Hey, everybody.

2
00:00:13.576 --> 00:00:16.138
I'm back to tell you about another landmark.

3
00:00:16.979 --> 00:00:19.821
But first check out this rainbow.

4
00:00:20.942 --> 00:00:21.843
Isn't it cool?

5
00:00:22.483 --> 00:00:25.325
It appeared right after the thunderstorm we just had!

6
00:00:26.386 --> 00:00:31.931
The rainbow in my backyard is amazing, but it's not as awesome as the ones that appear at

7
00:00:31.991 --> 00:00:34.753
Victoria Falls, the landmark Mr.

8
00:00:34.873 --> 00:00:36.553
Fay taught us about today.

9
00:00:37.734 --> 00:00:43.517
Victoria Falls is right on the border of Zimbabwe and Zambia in southern Africa.

10
00:00:44.617 --> 00:00:52.420
It's where the giant Zambezi River falls suddenly into a deep gorge, creating the world's largest curtain of

11
00:00:52.561 --> 00:00:53.501
falling water.

12
00:00:54.621 --> 00:00:59.824
Mr. Fay said Victoria Falls is one of the Seven Natural Wonders of the World!

13
00:01:01.732 --> 00:01:09.577
According to geologists, the Zambezi River has been sculpting the plains of Africa for about two million years.

14
00:01:10.837 --> 00:01:20.323
The incredible force of this 3,540-kilometer river cuts through weak areas of rock, creating cracks and gorges.

15
00:01:21.464 --> 00:01:28.408
Today the river tumbles from the edge of a grassy plain into a gorge, forming Victoria Falls.

16
00:01:29.712 --> 00:01:36.075
The gorge is only about 25 to 75 meters wide; if it weren't for the spray from the

17
00:01:36.155 --> 00:01:41.717
falls, from far away it might look like the water was just disappearing into the earth!

18
00:01:43.558 --> 00:01:50.881
The African name for the falls is Mosi-oa-Tunya, which means the "The Smoke that Thunders."

19
00:01:52.162 --> 00:01:56.464
If you ever visit this place, you'll easily see why it's named that.

20
00:01:57.640 --> 00:02:05.290
The spray from the falls—which can rise four hundred to eight hundred meters during the flood season—looks like

21
00:02:05.430 --> 00:02:07.733
smoke billowing up into the sky.

22
00:02:08.914 --> 00:02:12.358
It can be seen from up to twenty kilometers away!

23
00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:16.684
And the roar Well, let me tell you about the roar.

24
00:02:17.984 --> 00:02:25.987
Millions of liters of water crashing from a height of 108 meters sounds like thunder booming from about

25
00:02:26.107 --> 00:02:27.627
twenty storms at once!

26
00:02:29.508 --> 00:02:36.350
The odd thing is that Victoria Falls is in a part of Africa that experiences both wet and

27
00:02:36.430 --> 00:02:37.350
dry seasons.

28
00:02:38.491 --> 00:02:41.792
The wet season peaks around February or March.

29
00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:49.586
This is when you can see the 1,700-meter-wide curtain of water and its magnificent sprays.

30
00:02:50.907 --> 00:03:00.096
But during the dry season, from around May to November, the huge curtain disappears, and Victoria Falls turns

31
00:03:00.156 --> 00:03:02.398
into a series of small waterfalls.

32
00:03:03.619 --> 00:03:08.864
Mr. Fay told us that a lot of tourists have a hard time deciding when to visit.

33
00:03:10.088 --> 00:03:17.196
In the wet season, the powerful falls are awesome, but the spray is so high and thick you

34
00:03:17.216 --> 00:03:18.678
can hardly see anything!

35
00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:26.907
Some people choose to go in the dry season because the smaller waterfalls are still pretty, and the

36
00:03:26.967 --> 00:03:31.512
lower water levels reveal little islands that are upstream from the falls.

37
00:03:32.732 --> 00:03:38.456
You can even take boat tours to these islands and get an incredible view of the water as

38
00:03:38.516 --> 00:03:40.157
it disappears over the edge!

39
00:03:41.438 --> 00:03:47.162
One of these islands, Livingstone Island, is named for the Scottish explorer Dr.

40
00:03:47.242 --> 00:03:48.343
David Livingstone.

41
00:03:49.484 --> 00:03:57.129
In the 1850s Livingstone and his team were exploring the area around the Zambezi River when some locals

42
00:03:57.209 --> 00:04:00.832
told him that he must view the "Smoke That Thunders."

43
00:04:01.968 --> 00:04:10.515
In 1855 he became the first European to ever see the natural wonder, and he named it Victoria

44
00:04:10.575 --> 00:04:12.637
Falls, after Queen Victoria.

45
00:04:13.718 --> 00:04:17.200
Apparently he was stunned by the beauty of what he saw.

46
00:04:18.882 --> 00:04:26.548
In his travel journals, he wrote, "The whole scene was extremely beautiful; the banks and islands dotted over

47
00:04:26.588 --> 00:04:33.789
the river are adorned with great variety of color and form no one can imagine the beauty of

48
00:04:33.809 --> 00:04:42.854
the view And in his description of the water itself he wrote, "The snow-white sheet seemed like small

49
00:04:42.954 --> 00:04:49.678
comets rushing on in one direction, each of which left behind its rays of foam."

50
00:04:50.878 --> 00:04:55.141
Today there is a monument on Livingstone Island commemorating Dr.

51
00:04:55.181 --> 00:05:01.164
Livingstone, but what really draws tourists to the island is its closeness to Devil's Pool.

52
00:05:02.448 --> 00:05:09.014
Adventure-seekers can jump into the small but deep pool that sits right at the crest of the falls.

53
00:05:10.936 --> 00:05:16.541
From there you can look down over the falls while actually standing in the river.

54
00:05:17.782 --> 00:05:23.587
But if you're not a good swimmer, or the water is too high, you could get swept away

55
00:05:23.647 --> 00:05:26.109
and tumble down into the rocky gorge!

56
00:05:27.370 --> 00:05:33.906
So just for fun, let's pretend that you somehow manage to survive the hundred- meter drop into the

57
00:05:33.966 --> 00:05:41.850
gorge. After the long plunge, you'd end up in the Boiling Pot, a deep pool at the end

58
00:05:41.890 --> 00:05:47.573
of the gorge, along with the remains of hippos and zebras that have gotten carried off by the

59
00:05:47.633 --> 00:05:48.733
river during floods.

60
00:05:49.894 --> 00:05:54.536
But don't worry; the water in the Boiling Pot isn't really boiling.

61
00:05:55.708 --> 00:05:59.549
It just churns and foams, so it looks as if it's boiling.

62
00:06:01.290 --> 00:06:07.431
If you manage to flow free from the Boiling Pot, you'd end up traveling along the Zambezi River

63
00:06:07.711 --> 00:06:11.332
through Mozambique, all the way out to the Indian Ocean.

64
00:06:12.432 --> 00:06:15.293
There are many other ways to enjoy the falls.

65
00:06:16.394 --> 00:06:22.055
You can take a helicopter tour or fly over the river in a tiny, two-person airplane.

66
00:06:23.175 --> 00:06:31.114
You can also view the falls by foot, car, or train via the Victoria Falls Bridge, a single-span

67
00:06:31.194 --> 00:06:34.896
steel arch bridge connecting Zambia and Zimbabwe.

68
00:06:35.936 --> 00:06:42.659
Built in 1905, the bridge is still one of only a few spanning these two countries' borders.

69
00:06:43.860 --> 00:06:49.422
From the Victoria Falls Bridge, I bet you could get an excellent view of one of those big

70
00:06:49.562 --> 00:06:50.562
rainbows Mr.

71
00:06:50.622 --> 00:06:51.423
Fay showed us.

72
00:06:53.424 --> 00:06:54.104
Oh darn!

73
00:06:55.188 --> 00:06:57.510
The rainbow outside is gone already.

74
00:06:58.530 --> 00:07:01.413
But that's how most things are in nature, right?

75
00:07:02.433 --> 00:07:03.914
They're always changing.

76
00:07:04.935 --> 00:07:10.879
The Zambezi River doesn't look the same two years in a row, and Victoria Falls doesn't look the

77
00:07:10.919 --> 00:07:13.361
same in February as it does in November.

78
00:07:14.382 --> 00:07:20.706
But I hope the falls don't change too much because I want to hop into Devil's Pool one

79
00:07:20.766 --> 00:07:23.228
day! See you next week!
